[Music]  eleven-year-old Charlotte never as is  breaking racial barriers with her  impressive performance as Murray in the  Nutcracker  she is the first black young woman ever  to play that leading role in the New  York City Ballet's production former  ballet star Tony Williams has broken  barriers for more than half a century  his urban Nutcracker in Boston  incorporates dance styles from hip hop  to flamenco and Mexican folk more than  75 kids from different ethnic and  economic backgrounds are part of this  year's production it all supports the  mission diversity through dance the  first show it's like oh my god is this  going to you know fail but the applause  was just tremendous this holiday season  marks 19 years of Boston's urban  Nutcracker cast and crew as diverse as  the choreography and music you have  diversity and the dance styles diversity  and the music diversity in the cast on  the stage because we're a multiracial  multicultural cast and also in the  audience was that your intent or is that  how this has happened it was sort of a  dream it just happened the show's  creator Tony Williams became the Boston  ballets first african-american principal  dancer in the late 60s a driving force  in his desire to give opportunity to  underrepresented youth I started dance  late I was 16 but before that as a very  young kid I was in street gangs here in  Boston and getting into trouble so dance  sort of saved my life that's no little  thing that's true I some of the guys in  the gang I were but most of the guys are  dead or some went to jail so I was  really fortunate Jason Jordan a new  dancer in this year's show also  considers himself fortunate as a dancer  who comes from an urban neighborhood it  just made sense to me  it was like being home and since I was  trained classically it just molded it  together in a way that it felt really  natural for me  slow good boy Jordan teaches dance at a  Boston Public School in an underserved  community by recruiting some of his  students for the show he's given it new  flavor and his kids a new outlook what  have you found about bringing these kids  that maybe weren't exposed to dance  before into the fold here that's the  most important thing to me is having  these kids when they're done with me I  teach at a K through 8 when they're done  with eighth grade and they're beautiful  dancers where do they go now to have a  place yes and professional dancing it's  a place that didn't seem to exist for  Erika Lam when she was dancing as a  young girl in Boston Lam wasn't offered  the lead role of Clara in the Nutcracker  because she was black that's the first  time I realized that my skin color was  gonna be you know something I have to  think about you know if I wanted to do  ballet it was Boston in the late 70s  busing was going on racial tensions were  pretty much pretty high I mean but my  mom was brought into the director's  office and they said you know we love  your daughter she's very talented  but it's a period piece and this is  Boston and we just don't know really how  people are gonna react in this urban  rendition lamb is playing the role of  Clara's mother the stage ballet isn't  like 15th century aristocracy anymore  you know I mean anybody can do ballet  and you know and anybody could be a  family part of that family is a  wide-ranging group of young dancers show  of hands how many of you had seen the  classic Nutcracker before he started  doing urban Nutcracker every year my  parents would take well my mom and  grandma would take me to see the  Nutcracker  the Boston Ballet but then he came along  in my life  yeah so I see in his show I'm like mom I  got to do this it was just like  different for me and it made me want to  start doing it um my mom was like now  give me like hey do you want to do this  do you want to do the urban eye tracker  I was like I'll try it and I've been  here ever since  if you had to sum up in a couple words  what the experience of doing this show  is like for you what would you say  amazing incredible sharing that joy is  what makes it all worthwhile for Tony  Williams because of 3,000 people there  might be one little girl or one boy that  comes that is going to be transformed  and it's gonna save one life and that  the way it's saved your life yes yes yes  yeah and so that that's well that's why  I do it it's like I'm not a spring  chicken anymore and but it's the young  kids it's like how could I not do this  for them they look forward to it every  year and again almost 20 years of this  and in an effort for him to bring dance  to all kids there's a new program and a  dance apprenticeship that they're  launching in January and it will be for  kids who show promise but can't afford  high levels of dance training the goal  to have more professional dancers of  color I feel like we talked about this a  lot but it's the idea that when you see  somebody who looks like you doing  something suddenly you say I can do that  and especially with this and opening up  the styles of dance it's opened up the  minds to ballet because if you're  brought in by the tap or by flamenco and  then you see the ballet you may be  intrigued as well on that  I love Dana when Jason tells you that he  teaches kids and schools that are  underserved and that he gets them into  this program because it's one thing to  have a parent introduce you to George  Ballantine's the Nutcracker it's a night  here to have a teacher and bring you  along for the ride that's wonderful and  drink it almost goes back to the story  that you brought us earlier of what's  the next step so it's great that you  introduce them right but now how do they  take that next step and this is saying  yes this can be a career for you this  can be something that you think  about doing professional yeah I love it  love it great piece very well done  you

